{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And Pharaoh commanded on that day.</b> Meaning, specifically on that day he commanded to do so, and the reason was so that in the very intensity of the affliction — when they could no longer endure it — that thought of fear would be forgotten from them and they would turn to do their labor. And the discerning will understand that an affliction that is present makes one forget a future affliction, and likewise one that has already passed; this is something perceptible to the discerning regarding any matter. And he reckoned that one day would suffice for this purpose." "<b>Or</b> the verse may intend to hint that nothing remained for them of the labor except that day, for on the morrow Moses came and struck the river of Egypt, and the rod of bondage was broken, as they said, of blessed memory: from the time the plagues began, the bondage was removed from them, and moreover they began to spend their money for drinking water, as stated in their words, of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 9:10). And one should not raise a difficulty from his saying ״גַּם תְּמוֹל גַּם הַיּוֹם״, for the day before it they did not complete their quota, for it was a day of tidings and they were gathered before Moses who performed the signs before their eyes."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:50.152157674Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}